  "chapter": 840,
  "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 115 Chapter 840\n \n        MEMORANDUM filed with Senate Bill Number 8830, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n        the location of environmental facilities\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend  the  State  Environmental  Quality  Review  Act\n(SEQR)  and  Uniform  Procedures  Act (UPA) to require that the environ-\nmental review and permitting of facilities in a disadvantaged  community\n(DAC) assess whether such facilities may result in a disproportionate or\ninequitable  pollution  burden  on those communities. If permitting such\nfacility would result in a  disproportionate  or  inequitable  pollution\nburden on those communities, then the permit cannot be issued.\n\n  SEQR  applies  to  the decisions of thousands of State and local agen-\ncies, which include departments, boards,  public  benefit  corporations,\npublic  authorities,  commissions,  and  other governing bodies, and UPA\napplies to certain DEC permitting decisions.  With  regard  to  the  UPA\namendments  that would affect DEC permitting, the Climate Leadership and\nCommunity Protection Act requires that decisions made by State  agencies\ndo  not disproportionately burden those communities and further requires\nthat state agencies prioritize reductions of GHG emissions and  copollu-\ntants in those communities. DEC will continue to evaluate and update its\npolicies to ensure successful implementation of the provision.\n \n  The  intent  of  this  bill  is laudable. However as drafted, it would\nrequire significant State and local government  resources  to  implement\nand could lead to widespread confusion among the regulated community and\ntheir State and local regulators if it is not amended. It is also imper-\native  that  such  efforts do not impede the critical need to build more\nhousing.   I have secured an agreement  with  the  Legislature  to  make\namendments that will ensure we are protecting DACs from disproportionate\nand  harmful  pollution  burdens,  while balancing the need for critical\ninfrastructure such as  affordable  housing,  hospitals,  and  renewable\nfacilities  in  the State and ensuring such critical infrastructure does\nnot need to be removed, potentially harming  a  community  in  the  long\nterm.  These  amendments  will  also provide State and local governments\nsufficient time to update  their  current  procedures  and  programs  to\nimplement this new law.\n \n  On the basis of this agreement, I am signing this bill.\n \n  This bill is approved.                         (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
